Statistical Analysis Plan
Protocol No.: BAY 1841788 / 17777
Page: 18 of 48
The SAS code will be similar to the following:
PROC LIFETEST DATA=<DATASET>;
TIME EFFVAL * CENSORNY;
STRATA {strata variables} / GROUP=TREATMGR test=(logrank);
RUN;
The HR (darolutamide or placebo) for OS and its 95% confidence interval (CI) will be
calculated using the Cox model, stratified by the same factors as were used for randomization.
The SAS code similar to the following will be used:
PROC PHREG DATA = <DATASET>;
MODEL EFFVAL * CENSORNY = TREATMGR;
STRATA {strata variables};
RUN;
Kaplan-Meier (KM) estimates for the median time of OS (including 95% CI) and 25% and
75% percentiles will be presented for each treatment arm. The KM estimates at time points
such as 12 months, 24 months, etc., together with corresponding 95% CIs and the differences
of these estimates between the darolutamide+docetaxel arm and the placebo+docetaxel arm
will be presented. The SAS code will be similar to the following:
PROC LIFETEST DATA = <DATASET>;
TIME EFFVAL * CENSORNY;
STRATA TREATMGR;
RUN;
Descriptive statistics of survival follow-up time will be calculated by treatment arm and total.
Follow-up time is the time to LKAD or death as described for the OS endpoint for censored
and uncensored patients.
Sensitivity analyses
OS will be evaluated with the unstratified log-rank test and Cox model for the FAS population
(OS sensitivity analysis 1 - unstratified analysis).
OS will also be evaluated with the stratified log-rank test and Cox model for the FAS
population using stratification factors collected from the eCRF, in case there are more than
5% of patients with different values in any stratification variable between IxRS and eCRF (OS
sensitivity analysis 2 - eCRF-variables stratified analysis).
In addition, OS will also be evaluated with the stratified log-rank test and Cox model for the
FAS population using extent of disease stratification factors collected from the central
imaging review (OS sensitivity analysis 3 - central imaging review extent of disease as
stratification factor).
